\v 47 Any person who¦53654 lies¦53654 down¦53654 or eats¦53660 in¦53661 that house¦53661, must¦53662 wash¦53662 their clothes¦53665.
\p
\v 48 \add ≈However,\add* if¦53667 the¦53671 priest¦53671 \add ≈came¦53670 back\add* after the¦53677 house¦53678 was¦53680 replastered and \add ≈after his inspection, find that\add* the¦53685 growth \add ≈hasn't reappeared\add*, then \add *he\add* can pronounce the¦53688 house¦53688 as ‘clean¦53684’ because¦53689 the¦53691 \add ≈growth has¦53690 gone\add*.
\v 49 Then¦53694 he¦53694 must¦53694 take¦53694 two¦53699 birds¦53700, cedar¦53702 wood, scarlet¦53703 \add ≈thread\add*, and¦53705 some hyssop¦53705 \add twigs\add*,
\v 50 and¦53707 slaughter one¦53711 bird¦53710 \add ?≈so the¦53711 blood runs into¦53712\add* a¦53714 clay¦53716 jug \add ?≈containing\add* water¦53719 \add ≈from a spring or creek\add*.
\v 51 Then¦53722 \add ?@the¦53722 priest¦53722\add* must¦53722 take¦53722 the¦53725 live bird, along with the¦53727 cedar¦53727 wood¦53725, the¦53733 scarlet¦53733 \add thread\add*, the¦53734 hyssop¦53730 \add twigs\add* and¦53735 the¦53736 live bird¦53736, and¦53738 dip¦53738 them¦53739 \add ≈all\add* into¦53740 that¦53742 blood¦53740 of¦53740 the¦53741 slaughtered bird¦53741 (\add ?≈mixed with\add* the¦53742 \add ≈fresh\add* water¦53743), and¦53745 \add ≈flick\add* it seven¦53749 times¦53750 onto the¦53748 house¦53748.
\v 52 \add In that way,\add* he'll¦53752 purify¦53752 the¦53755 house¦53755 with¦53756 the¦53756 bird's blood¦53756 and the \add ≈fresh\add* water¦53758, and¦53760 with¦53760 the¦53761 live¦53761 bird and¦53762 the¦53763 cedar¦53763 \add wood\add*, the hyssop¦53764 \add twigs\add* and¦53765 the¦53765 scarlet¦53765 \add thread\add*.
