\p
\v 10 \add ≈However,\add* no stranger¦57486 may eat¦57489 the sacred¦57490 \add >food\add*, \add e.g.,\add* someone staying with the priest¦57492, or his hired¦57493 servant.
\v 11 If¦57500 the priest¦57499 had purchased \add ≈the person¦57503\add* with \add ≈cash¦57505\add*, then \add @that person\add* can eat¦57507 it¦57508, plus¦57509 anyone¦57509 that was born¦57509 into \add @the¦57510 priest's¦57510\add* \add ≈household¦57510\add*.
\v 12 A¦57515 priest's daughter¦57515 who \add ≈is married by\add* \add ≈someone who's not a¦57517 priest¦57517\add* can \add no longer\add* eat¦57526 those sacred¦57524 \add ≈offerings¦57523\add*.
\v 13 \add However, in the future\add* if¦57531 she¦57532 became a¦57533 widow¦57533 or was divorced¦57534 without having had children¦57535, and¦57538 if she¦57538 has returned¦57538 to¦57539 her¦57542 father's¦57542 house¦57541 like¦57543 when she was young¦57543, then she can eat \add ≈the¦57544 same food¦57544 as her¦57545 father¦57545\add*, \add ≈but¦57547 no\add* stranger¦57549 may eat¦57552 it¦57553.
\p
\v 14 If¦57557 someone¦57556 \add ≈accidentally¦57561\add* eats¦57559 something that's sacred¦57560, then¦57562 they must¦57562 \add ≈pay back the full value plus¦57565 20%\add* to¦57566 the priest¦57566.
